To improve iNGenDNS, you should focus on closing the technical gaps mentioned in the README, expanding modern DNS protocol support, and enhancing the Android system integration.Here is a roadmap of specific features, architecture enhancements, and optimizations you can implement:
1. Close Core VPN & Networking GapsAdd TCP Fallback: Large DNS responses (like heavy TXT records or DNSSEC data) trigger a truncation flag (TC=1) over UDP, requiring a TCP fallback. Implement a local TCP listener to accept, forward, and parse these streams.Implement IPv6 Forwarding: The app currently filters out IPv6 DNS traffic. Expand your VpnService to capture IPv6 UDP traffic (port 53) and route it to your validated IPv6 resolvers.Network-Change Handling: Enhance ConnectivityManager.NetworkCallback to seamlessly tear down and rebuild the VPN tunnel without packet leaks when switching between Wi-Fi and Cellular data.
  2. Expand Modern DNS Protocol SupportDNS-over-TLS (DoT) & DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH): Raw UDP DNS is prone to eavesdropping. Add support to encapsulate the captured local UDP traffic into upstream DoT (port 853) or DoH (port 443) requests using libraries like OkHttp for DoH.DNS-over-QUIC (DoQ): Implement DoQ for upstream resolvers to achieve lower handshake latencies and better performance on unstable mobile connections.
  3. Advance the Benchmarking EngineSmart Background Scheduling: Instead of a rigid 6-hour WorkManager window, trigger a benchmark dynamically when the device connects to a new Wi-Fi SSID or cell tower ID, ensuring the benchmarks are always relevant to the current network infrastructure.Domain Diversity: Benchmarking with only a single standard query can skew results due to caching. Query a rotating pool of popular domains (e.g., Google, GitHub, Wikipedia) during the 10-sample cycle to get a true latency and packet-loss profile.
    4. Improve User Experience & OS IntegrationQuick Settings Tile: Add an Android Quick Settings Tile to let users quickly toggle DNS Optimization on and off without opening the dashboard.Dynamic Material 3 Color: Ensure your Jetpack Compose dashboard pulls from the system's dynamic user theme (Monet) for a native Android look and feel.Per-App Tunnelling: Use VpnService.Builder.addAllowedApplication() or addDisallowedApplication() to allow users to bypass the DNS tunnel for specific apps (e.g., corporate apps or banking apps that break under custom DNS).
      5. F-Droid Lifecycle AcceptanceAddress Trackers & Metadata: Since you have opened an F-Droid Request For Packaging (RFP), double-check that your fdroidRelease flavor strictly omits any transitive Google dependencies. Ensure your Fastlane metadata is complete to prevent build scanner errors.If you would like to work on a specific part first, let me know:Do you want to start with the upstream DoH/DoT implementation?Would you prefer to write the code for the IPv6 / TCP local forwarder?Should we design the Jetpack Compose Quick Settings Tile?
